{% karta "wiemy", i18n[jezyk].zakladki.wiemy %}
<p>A stucco sundial went up on the garden front of Wilanów Palace, on the south-eastern corner pavilion, around 1684. {% zrodlo "milewska-2010", "A museum study" %} describes not one dial but three: in the centre a sundial with Roman numerals showing equal hours, on the left an Italian dial counted from the last sunset, on the right a Babylonian one counted from sunrise. Three reckonings of the same moment, each starting the day somewhere else.</p>
<p>The pointers are part of the sculpture, not rods driven into the wall: the centre dial's pointer is an ear of grain held by Saturn, and the pointers of both side dials are held by putti.</p>

{{ rownowaznik | safe }}

<p>That the palace clocks were his business at all is attested in the scholarship: {% zrodlo "fijalkowski-1989", "Wojciech Fijałkowski, Studia Wilanowskie XI" %} records — after Juliusz Starzyński's monograph — that Kochański “was in charge of compasses and clocks for the palace”, and that he was the main author of the ideological programme of the decoration of the palace elevation and interiors. The article says nothing about the sundial itself, however, and never mentions {% objasnij "Hevelius", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Johannes_Hevelius", "Johannes Hevelius, astronomer of Gdańsk" %}.</p>

<p class="note">A photograph of the surviving dial with a description of its medallion: {% zrodlo "widacka-2008", "an article by Hanna Widacka for the museum" %} (in Polish). The photograph is not freely licensed, so it opens at the publisher's site, in a separate tab.</p>


<p class="note uwaga">The model simplifies the monument in three ways at once. It assumes a wall facing due south, it replaces the three sculpted pointers with a single rod perpendicular to the wall — one sun, one shadow, three grids for that shadow to fall on — and it does not reproduce the stucco decoration. The wall's real declination, and what each of the three pointers is geometrically, are item A1 under “Open questions”.</p>

